  "scope": [
    "Seven selected native Linux network devices retain complete before/after IPv6 OUTPUT rules, actual interfaces/routes, libc identity, resolver/NSS files and listening sockets/processes. New leading UDP53/TCP53 REJECT rules extend the prior IPv4 prefix controls.",
    "Two actual FreeBSD Inners retain effective enabled PF state, complete state tables, local addresses, full rules/config and libc/socket inventories. Existing source-self rules cover conventional TCP/UDP53; no arbitrary encrypted-DNS restriction is asserted. Current state presence is an independent condition and must be checked from retained records.",
    "The original v8 FreeBSD Python probes fail because that guest does not install Python. The v9 helper uses the already installed drill tool with explicit IPv6 and UDP/TCP selection. The earlier Linux loopback probes encountered disabled IPv6 and did not increment rule counters. The v10 helper explicitly enables only IPv6 loopback and assigns ::1/128 before testing. External interface IPv6 settings are unchanged. All final IPv6 probes target the actual loopback IPv6 address ::1. Rule counters before/after distinguish the active rejection match from lack of a listening server. No externally routed IPv6 path is claimed. Positive nested Red traffic is separately tested in both directions.",
    "Musl-based routers identify musl 1.2.5 through the loader diagnostic; GNU getconf and ldd version-query failures are retained. An nsswitch hosts line alone is not asserted to change musl resolver behavior. Actual empty resolver files, packet filters and listening-service inventory remain independent observations. Host root, Docker, guest-root configuration and key provisioning remain trusted controls. The service/resolver inventories are point observations. Default accounts, organizational policies, all future applications and remote boot defaults remain separate clauses."
  ]
